<repaired_code>
def find_roots(
    graph: "Graph", prop: "URIRef", roots: Optional[Set["Node"]] = None
) -> Set["Node"]:
    # If roots parameter is provided, use it; otherwise start with all subjects
    if roots is not None:
        candidates = roots
    else:
        # Get all nodes that appear as either subject or object
        candidates = set()
        for s, o in graph.subject_objects(prop):
            candidates.add(s)
            candidates.add(o)
        
    if not candidates:
        return set()
    
    # Find all nodes that have a parent
    non_roots = set()
    for s, o in graph.subject_objects(prop):
        non_roots.add(s)  # If it has a parent, it's not a root
        
    # Return nodes that don't have any parents
    return candidates - non_roots
</repaired_code>

This implementation:
1. Takes an optional set of root candidates
2. If no roots are provided, collects all nodes involved in the property relationship
3. Identifies nodes that have parents (appear as subjects in triples)
4. Returns the set of nodes that don't have parents (the roots)

The code now properly handles the transitive hierarchy and follows the RDFS.subClassOf or SKOS.broader pattern mentioned in the docstring, where triples are of the form (child, prop, parent).

